From: edde746 <86283021+edde746@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] demux_mkv: let trailing Tags wait for the Cues on network streams

demux_mkv_open() defers the Cues only when they are the one level 1
element behind the clusters. mkvmerge writes the Cues and then the Tags
there, so every file it muxes was opened with a seek to the end of the
file and one back to the first cluster. Over HTTP each of those is a new
connection (three before the first packet, four when playback resumes at
a saved position), and the last one fetches the first cluster only to
drop it for the resume seek.

On a network stream the Tags now wait with the Cues
(--demuxer-mkv-defer-tags, default yes): opening such a file reads
nothing behind the clusters, and the first seek reads the Cues and, on
the way, the Tags that follow them. Tags are never fetched on their own,
so a file that never seeks, or whose index sits in front, does not read
them. Read that late, only the tags of the file itself can still be
published (as a metadata update); track, chapter and edition tags are
dropped, because the player reads those structures without locking once
the file is open. mkvmerge's Tags are its per-track statistics (BPS,
DURATION, NUMBER_OF_FRAMES ...), which mpv only passes on as track
metadata. Local files, files with their Tags in front (ffmpeg's muxer),
anything else behind the clusters (chapters, attachments), --replaygain
other than no (its values may live in the Tags), and
--demuxer-mkv-defer-tags=no read what they read before.

Measured with netbench.py (real TCP and TLS 1.3 through a relay that
delays each direction by RTT/2), a 2 h 10 Mbit/s mkvmerge file, time
from mpv opening the URL to "playback restart complete", median (range)
of 3 interleaved runs, connections in brackets:

  RTT 40 ms   open at 0     443 (438-451) [3] -> 180 (178-182) [1]
              resume 30 min 661 (620-667) [4] -> 509 (506-524) [3]
  RTT 100 ms  open at 0     974 (974-999) [3] -> 391 (366-420) [1]
              resume 30 min 1356 (1354-1362) [4] -> 1093 (1058-1099) [3]

The first seek after an open at 0 now reads the Cues: at 40 ms it takes
487 (470-506) ms on two connections instead of 381 (358-387) on one.
With the kept connections of mpv patch 0241 that seek is one connection
and two requests (449 ms), and the resume two connections (416 ms).

A seek to a header element that fails is now tried once more on a
network stream. One refused or dropped connection used to leave the file
without its index (before this patch too, when the Cues were read while
opening), and a seek then builds one by reading the file from its start:
with the first connection to the Cues closed unanswered, a resume at
30 min took 24.4 s and 2.3 GB on loopback, and takes 0.7 s now.

demux_metadata_changed() now asks demux_update() for a merge outright.
demux_update() tells a change by looking up the entries for the last and
the current playback position in the list as it is at that moment, so an
entry that arrives at or before the position playback is at was the answer
for both and never reached the metadata. That is where a seek puts the
Tags it reads: the ones a resume (--start) read were published only if
the player happened not to poll between the seek and their arrival
(iPhone, loopback http with 50 ms per response: "metadata" stayed empty
in 12 of 12 resumes, and has the file's tags at the restart in 6 of 6 now).

test_demux_mkv_defer_tags.sh replays what opening and the first seek
read for each layout.

diff --git a/DOCS/man/options.rst b/DOCS/man/options.rst
index 97ad814..817290d 100644
--- a/DOCS/man/options.rst
+++ b/DOCS/man/options.rst
@@ -4143,6 +4143,19 @@ Demuxer
     also reads the first timestamp, which may increase latency by one frame
     (which may be relevant for live streams).
 
+``--demuxer-mkv-defer-tags=<yes|no>``
+    On a network stream, do not read Tags stored after the clusters when
+    opening the file (default: yes). mkvmerge writes its Tags there, behind the
+    Cues, and reading them on opening costs a seek to the end of the file and
+    one back before the first packet: over HTTP, two more connections. With
+    this option they are read on the way when the first seek reads the Cues.
+    Until then, and for good if nothing seeks, their values are missing from
+    ``metadata``. Track, chapter and edition tags (such as mkvmerge's per-track
+    statistics) read that late are not published at all, since those cannot
+    change once the file is open. Local files, files whose Tags precede the
+    clusters, and playback with ``--replaygain`` enabled always read them on
+    opening.
+
 ``--demuxer-mkv-probe-video-duration=<yes|no|full>``
     When opening the file, seek to the end of it, and check what timestamp the
     last video packet has, and report that as file duration. This is strictly
diff --git a/demux/demux.c b/demux/demux.c
index 86a233d..7032b87 100644
--- a/demux/demux.c
+++ b/demux/demux.c
@@ -3090,6 +3090,11 @@ void demux_metadata_changed(demuxer_t *demuxer)
 
     mp_mutex_lock(&in->lock);
     add_timed_metadata(in, demuxer->metadata, NULL, MP_NOPTS_VALUE);
+    // demux_update() finds a change by looking up the entries for the last
+    // and the current position in the list as it is now. An entry that lands
+    // at or before the position playback is at (Matroska Tags read by a
+    // seek) is the answer for both from then on, and would never be merged.
+    in->force_metadata_update = true;
     mp_mutex_unlock(&in->lock);
 }
 
